The exhaustive review finds one decisive unsound rule: the whole-program bridge at `semantic.k:119–121`. The arithmetic/filter/sort equations are internally consistent, but the entry postcondition reduces to those same equations, and there is no bridge-free evaluator or connection theorem. The 995-digit positive witness gives the required false conclusion: K proves a normal return that the actual submitted body does not produce. The `writing-semantics` requirement materially fails because declarations exist for every AST constructor but none of their behaviors do.
The actual translated term is syntactically pinned, but source constructs are never evaluated. Exhaustive inventory identifies the exact-program summary rule as an unjustified operational bridge with an intended-domain false-conclusion witness.
  ✓ Stage 1: verify launcher provenance, record layout, hashes, mounts, and generated-semantics boundary
  ✓ Stage 2: verify source/translation fidelity and run independent differential tests
  ✓ Stage 3: reconstruct concrete semantics and all proof claims from clean scratch
  ✓ Stage 4: audit theorem adequacy and real-program pinning
  ✓ Stage 5: inventory and review every local syntax/function/rule/declaration
  → Stage 6: build and run a fresh false result mutation
  • Stage 7: account for proven versus assumed facts and write REVIEW.md verdict
